App/SampleData/aava-n2k-3min.jsonl
  The first 3 minutes of the NMEA 2000 sample recording "aava-n2k.data" from the
  Signal K server project (https://github.com/SignalK/signalk-server), converted to
  Signal K deltas with canboatjs and n2k-signalk.
  Signal K server, canboatjs and n2k-signalk are licensed under the Apache License 2.0.
  The values "steering.rudderAngle" and "steering.rudderAngleTarget" (source
  "simulated.rudder") are not part of the recording. They are simulated by
  Scripts/add-simulated-rudder.py.
  The own "navigation.rateOfTurn" values (source "simulated.rateOfTurn") are not part of the
  recording either. They are calculated from the recorded heading by
  Scripts/add-simulated-rate-of-turn.py.
  The engine values "propulsion.port.*" and "propulsion.starboard.*" (source
  "simulated.engines") are not part of the recording. They are simulated by
  Scripts/add-simulated-engines.py. They include the lever position "revolutionsTarget",
  which is not a standard Signal K path.
  The first line (a Signal K hello message) declares the own vessel as MMSI 230035780.
  This is deduced from the data: that AIS target reports the same position as the own GPS,
  so it is the own transponder.

App/Shoreline/shoreline.dysl
  Coastline from GSHHG 2.3.7 (Global Self-consistent, Hierarchical, High-resolution
  Geography Database) by Paul Wessel and Walter H. F. Smith,
  https://www.soest.hawaii.edu/pwessel/gshhg/
  GSHHG is distributed under the GNU Lesser General Public License version 3 or later.
  Its copyright and permission notice is in App/Shoreline/GSHHG-LICENSE.txt, and the licence
  texts are in App/Shoreline/COPYING.LESSERv3.txt and App/Shoreline/COPYINGv3.txt.
  The file is converted from gshhs_f.b (full resolution) by Scripts/make-shoreline.py:
  only the sea coast and the Antarctic ice front are kept, cut into 1° cells and
  slightly simplified. The rest of DashYacht stays under the MIT licence.
  The shoreline is for display only. It is not precise enough for navigation.
